@page "/HowItWorks/interest"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/interest">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Types of interest entry</h2>
            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr><th>Type</th><th>Where it comes from</th><th>How the app uses it</th></tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr><td>Saving interest income</td><td>Interest on cash from any broker</td><td>Taxable interest. For Interactive Brokers it is assumed to come from the UK.</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Bond coupon</td><td>Interactive Brokers bond coupon payments</td><td>Taxable interest, with the country of the bond's issuer</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Accrued income profit or loss</td><td>Accrued interest when you sell or buy a bond</td><td>Taxable or deductible under the accrued income scheme (see below)</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>ETF dividend income</td><td>Fund dividends you chose to treat as interest</td><td>Taxable interest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Excess Reportable Income (Interest)</td><td>Added on the ERI and Equalisation page</td><td>Taxable interest</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Interest expense (not deductible)</td><td>Margin interest you paid to Interactive Brokers</td><td>Listed for reference only and left out of all totals</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
            <p>
                Margin interest is not deducted because, if you report capital gains and do not run your investing as a
                trade, it is normally not an allowable expense. Interest received while you are not UK resident is left out, as
                for <a href="HowItWorks/dividends" class="link-styled">dividends</a>.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Accrued income on bonds</h2>
            <p>
                When you buy or sell a bond at a "dirty" price, the price includes interest built up since the last coupon.
                The seller has an accrued income profit and the buyer an accrued income loss. These are taxed or deducted
                when the next coupon is paid, not when you trade.
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    The app looks for the next coupon on the same bond after the trade. If that coupon is paid in the same tax
                    year, the accrued income counts in that year.
                </li>
                <li>
                    If the next coupon falls in a later tax year, or there is no later coupon in your files, the accrued income
                    is moved to the following tax year.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Check the result on the <a href="DividendDataPage" class="link-styled">Dividend/Income Data</a> page, for
                example if you have not yet imported the statement with the next coupon.
            </p>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/accrued-income-scheme-hs343-self-assessment-helpsheet" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC HS343: Accrued Income Scheme</a></li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/dividends" class="link-styled">Dividends</a>: including fund dividends treated as interest</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
